  "working": "Let $P(x) = (x + 3)^6 (3x - 1)^5 = \\sum_{k=0}^{11} a_k x^k$.\n\nThe sum of the coefficients of the odd powers of $x$ is given by:\n$$S_{\\text{odd}} = a_1 + a_3 + a_5 + a_7 + a_9 + a_{11} = \\dfrac{P(1) - P(-1)}{2}$$\n\nStep 1: Evaluate $P(1)$:\n$$P(1) = (1 + 3)^6 (3(1) - 1)^5 = 4^6 \\cdot 2^5 = (2^2)^6 \\cdot 2^5 = 2^{12} \\cdot 2^5 = 2^{17}$$\n\nStep 2: Evaluate $P(-1)$:\n$$P(-1) = (-1 + 3)^6 (3(-1) - 1)^5 = 2^6 \\cdot (-4)^5 = 2^6 \\cdot (-(2^2)^5) = 2^6 \\cdot (-2^{10}) = -2^{16}$$\n\nStep 3: Compute $S_{\\text{odd}}$:\n$$S_{\\text{odd}} = \\dfrac{2^{17} - (-2^{16})}{2} = \\dfrac{2^{17} + 2^{16}}{2} = 2^{16} + 2^{15} = 2^{15}(2 + 1) = 3 \\cdot 2^{15}$$",
  "distractor_notes": "$2^{15}$: Obtained by either making a sign error on $(-4)^5 = +2^{10}$ giving $\\frac{2^{17}-2^{16}}{2}$, or mistakenly computing the sum of even-power coefficients $\\frac{P(1)+P(-1)}{2}$.\n$2^{16}$: Obtained by assuming the sum of odd coefficients is simply $\\frac{1}{2}P(1) = \\frac{2^{17}}{2} = 2^{16}$ (a common misconception carried over from $(1+x)^n$).\n$3 \\cdot 2^{16}$: Obtained by correctly computing $P(1) - P(-1) = 3 \\cdot 2^{16}$ but forgetting to divide by $2$.",
